ABSTRACT 
Early Parkinson’s Disease Detection from Gait Using an 
Explainable CNN-Transformer and Early Gait Instability Index 
Parkinson's Disease (PD) is a progressive neurodegenerative condition that manifests 
with characteristic gait abnormalities, including decreased stride variability, asymmetry, 
resting tremor, and impaired turning. As PD symptoms are predominantly motor in nature, 
early-stage identification remains clinically challenging. This project introduces an 
automated, objective framework for early PD detection through an Early Gait Instability 
Index (EGI) using wearable inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensors placed at the ankles.  
The EGI derives five composite scores — variability, asymmetry, tremor, timing, and 
turning from the IMU recordings in the WearGait-PD dataset during free-paced walking and 
Timed Up-and-Go (TUG) activities, providing clinicians with interpretable, quantitative gait 
assessments. In addition to the EGI based SVM model, multiple deep learning models were 
developed and evaluated. The SVM classifier was trained on EGI features and demonstrated 
stronger predictive performance compared to a baseline classifier without EGI-derived 
features. Three deep learning models were also implemented: a 1D Residual CNN with 
Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) attention, a Hybrid CNN-Transformer, and a ResNet-18 trained 
on 2D Recurrence Plot (RP) gait images. All models were evaluated using subject-level 
group 5-fold cross-validation to prevent data leakage and reduce bias. SHAP explainability 
analysis was applied to validate and interpret the final results. The Hybrid CNN-Transformer 
achieved the best overall performance, attaining an AUC of 0.8544, accuracy of 79.87%, 
sensitivity of 80.88%, and specificity of 79.01%.  
