Chapter 1 Introduction 
1.1 Background and Motivation 
Parkinson's Disease (PD) is the second most prevalent neurodegenerative disorder in the 
world with over 10 million people worldwide affected [1]. It results from the progressive loss 
of dopamine-producing cells in the substantia nigra of the brain and causes symptoms of 
bradykinesia, rigidity, resting tremor and postural instability [2]. These symptoms create 
significant impairments in mobility, balance and quality of life. 
Gait problems are among the most frequent symptoms of Parkinson's. Patients may have a 
slow gait, a small step length, a poor turning ability, freezing of gait, and a loss of balance 
[3]. These abnormalities make people more likely to fall and be unable to walk around on 
their own. Gait-related symptoms often occur in early disease and gait analysis is a key 
research field for the early detection of Parkinson's Disease. 
The diagnosis of Parkinson's disease is largely based on clinical diagnosis and neurological 
examination. Early symptoms are, however, often subtle and hard to be accurately diagnosed 
[4]. Hence, researchers have been increasingly interested in wearable sensor technologies and 
in the use of artificial intelligence techniques for objective and automatic evaluation of 
Parkinson's disease. 
Portable, low-cost, and capable of continuous data acquisition of motion signals like 
acceleration and angular velocity, Wearable Inertial Measurement Unit (IMU) sensors are 
widely used in gait analysis [5]. IMU-based systems can be used to automatically detect gait 
abnormalities linked to Parkinson's disease in combination with machine learning and deep 
learning algorithms. 
This project aims to develop an explainable IMU-based gait classification model for detecting 
Parkinson's disease in the early stages with sensors placed on both of the ankles. The 
proposed system examines both the clinically engineered Explainable Gait Index (EGI) 
features and deep learning models like 1D-CNN, CNN-Transformer and ResNet 
architectures. In addition, explainability techniques like SHAP analysis and attention 
visualization are integrated for enhanced clinical interpretability. Early diagnosis of 
Parkinson's disease can help facilitate timely intervention, better management of the patient, 
and better quality of life [6]. 
6 
1.2 Purpose and Goal of the Project 
The main goal of this project is to create a precise and interpretable machine learning system 
to detect Parkinson's Disease (PD) in its early stages by analyzing gait using wearable 
sensors. The goal of the system is to automatically categorize Parkinsonian gait patterns from 
multichannel inertial measurement unit (IMU) signals collected using bilateral 
ankle-mounted sensors. 
Specifically , this project is aimed at the distinction of early-stage PD patients (Hoehn and 
Yahr stages 1-2) from healthy controls by analysing gait characteristics in spatiotemporal 
dimension. This is an automatic and objective evaluation of gait abnormalities, which is 
different from conventional clinical assessments, which are subjective and time consuming.  
A new Explainable Gait Index (EGI) is presented which encompasses five clinically 
interpretable components – variability, asymmetry, tremor, timing and turning – to quantify 
gait instability. The index is a single composite biomarker that reflects the severity of motor 
impairment as well as gait irregularity in Parkinson's disease.  
The major contributions of this work include the following: 
1. Proposing a new Early Gait Instability Index (EGI) : A novel Early Gait Index(EGI) 
was introduced which quantify gait instability by integrating five clinically 
interpretable components such as: Variability, Asymmetry, Tremor, Timing and 
Turning. EGI acts as a unified composite biomarker indicating the level of motor 
impairment and gait instability in PD patients. 
2. Multi-modal gait representation framework: The proposed framework utilizes raw 
IMU sensor signals along with Explainable Gait Index features to jointly represent 
gait at both low-level and high-level. 
3. Evaluation of several ML & DL algorithms: Traditional machine learning algorithms 
such as SVM were compared with Deep Learning algorithms including 
one-dimensional CNN, CNN-Transformer models, and ResNet-18 with 2D 
Recurrence Plot architectures in this study to perform classification for Parkinson’s 
Disease. 
4.  Focus on early stage PD: The research concentrated on the diagnosis of early stage 
Parkinson’s disease (H and Y Stage1-2) for its greater clinical challenges in detection. 
7 
5. Robust Subject-level Group 5-fold Cross-validation: A highly effective subject-level 
5-fold cross-validation method was used to avoid any possible data leakage during 
testing. 
6. Model explainability: SHAP-based feature importance and Attention 
mechanism-based visualization techniques were used to improve model 
explainability. 
7.  
Bilateral IMU sensors for gait asymmetry and turning evaluation: Bilateral ankle 
placement is useful for modeling interlimb asymmetry, turn performance, and gait 
coordination disorders. 
