Chapter 2 Research Literature Review 
2.1 Existing Research and Limitations 
1. Dataset Foundation ( WearGait-PD) 
The WearGait-PD open-access dataset used in this project was published in 2026 by 
Anderson et al. [1]. The dataset contains raw IMU and sensorized insole data from 100 
people with PD and 85 age-matched controls, recorded and synchronized to a gait walkway 
reference system, consisting of 3-DOF acceleration and rotational velocity from 13 
body-worn IMUs. All data were linked to clinical assessments based on H and Y scores and 
MDS-UPDRS, developed with the assistance of the FDA, VA, and Johns Hopkins School of 
Medicine. 
2. Classical Feature-Based Detection (CoP Insole Approach) 
Nanayakkara et al. [2] tested center-of-pressure (CoP) features extracted from wearable 
pressure insoles applied to the same WearGait-PD dataset for PD detection. The authors 
selected 39 PD and 38 controls and extracted 144 CoP features from four domains which are: 
positional, dynamic, frequency, and stochastic during the Timed Up and Go (TUG) test. A 
23-feature subset showed the best performance with logistic regression, yielding accuracy 
0.875 and ROC-AUC 0.921, where dynamic and frequency-domain asymmetry features were 
most frequently selected. 
3. Deep Learning with Image Representation (GAF-CNN) 
Deep Convolutional Networks (DCNNs) and Gramian Angular Fields (GAFs) were used by 
Mostafavi et al. [3] to classify PD gait signals from pressure insoles. Results showed 
accuracy of 98.6% and true positive rate of 99.2%, with severity estimated from 10-second 
gait windows achieving R² greater than 0.8 for Hoehn and Yahr scores. While the results 
appear strong, the dataset and evaluation methodology are not fully specified, limiting 
reproducibility and direct comparison. 
9 
4. Hybrid CNN-Transformer for PD Staging (VGRF Approach) 
Naimi et al. [4] proposed a hybrid 1D ConvNet-Transformer model to diagnose PD severity 
using VGRF signals. The proposed architecture combines CNN and Transformer in an 
end-to-end model, where the CNN extracts relevant local features from the VGRF signal and 
the Transformer captures long-term spatio-temporal dependencies. The model achieved 
87.89% accuracy on the Physionet gait dataset, outperforming SVM-RBF (75.60%), ANN 
(76.08%), and standalone 1D-CNN (85.30%) baselines. An ablation study confirmed that 
removing either the CNN or Transformer component reduced performance, showing both are 
essential. 
5. Transformer Architecture ( Self-Attention for Sequence Modelling) 
Vaswani et al. [5] introduced the Transformer which is a neural network architecture built 
entirely on self-attention mechanisms. The Transformer outperforms recurrent models in 
parallelizability and sequence modelling tasks. Multi-head attention enables the model to 
attend to information from various representation subspaces simultaneously at multiple 
positions, facilitating richer temporal modelling. This foundational architecture directly 
underpins our Hybrid CNN-Transformer, where a CLS token aggregates gait information 
across 50 temporal positions of each 2-second window. 
6. Channel Attention ( Squeeze-and-Excitation Networks) 
Hu et al. [6] introduced Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) blocks which adaptively recalibrate 
feature responses across CNN channels. SE blocks explicitly model interdependencies 
between channels, achieving significant performance improvements at minimal additional 
computational cost. In this project, SE blocks are integrated into the 1D-CNN and Hybrid 
CNN-Transformer to dynamically emphasise the most discriminative of the 128 learned gait 
features per input window which is an architectural improvement over the basic CNN used by 
Naimi et al. [4] which did not include channel attention. 
7. Explainable AI (SHAP Framework) 
Lundberg and Lee [7] proposed SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations)  which is a unified 
framework for interpreting machine learning predictions. SHAP assigns a significance score 
to each feature for a specific prediction, addressing the balance between accuracy and 
10 
interpretability in complex models. In this project, SHAP KernelExplainer is applied to the 
trained EGI-SVM model, providing clinical explainability not considered by Naimi et al. [4] 
or Mostafavi et al. [3], and revealing that gait variability contributes 61.8% of the total model 
decision weight. 
2.2 Limitations of Existing Work 
The following observations have been made after a detailed examination of the literature: 
(i) Use of pressure insoles or VGRF sensors instead of ankle IMU. Nanayakkara et al. [2] 
and Mostafavi et al. [3] employed pressure insoles, while Naimi et al. [4] used VGRF foot 
sensors from the Physionet dataset. Angular velocity, step asymmetry, and rotational 
dynamics have not been explored in prior work, and to our knowledge, limited study has been 
conducted using bilateral ankle IMU channels for PD classification on WearGait-PD. 
(ii) No clinically interpretable feature framework. Mostafavi et al. [3] used end-to-end 
deep learning with no clinical interpretation at the feature level. Nanayakkara et al. [2] 
derived generic CoP statistics without clinical motivation. Naimi et al. [4] processed raw 
VGRF signals with no clinical signal intention. None grounded their features in specific PD 
gait markers such as variability, asymmetry, tremor, timing, and turning. 
(iii) No explainable AI validation. Although SHAP [7] and Transformer attention [5] are 
established explainability tools, none of the reviewed studies applied them to validate model 
decisions against clinical gait knowledge. Naimi et al. [4] conducted only an ablation study 
without feature-level or attention-level clinical interpretation. 
(iv) Evaluation methodology limitations. Mostafavi et al. [3] reported 98.6% accuracy 
without subject-level Group cross-validation, risking data leakage. Naimi et al. [4] applied 
10-fold cross-validation at the walk-segment level rather than the subject level, allowing 
segments from the same subject to appear in both training and test folds. Nanayakkara et al. 
[2] used a single 80/20 split without Group K-Fold. 
(v) Multi-class severity staging rather than early-stage binary detection. Naimi et al. [4] 
targeted multi-class H and Y staging (Healthy/Mild/Medium/High) on moderate-to-severe 
patients (H and Y 2–3). No reviewed study addressed binary PD vs Control detection 
specifically for early-stage patients (H and Y 1–2),  the most clinically critical and least 
studied group. 
11 
(vi) No H and Y stage-stratified analysis. No reviewed study evaluated performance 
separately for H and Y Stage 1 and Stage 2 patients. Combining all early PD stages hides 
whether detection works for the mildest patients who need early intervention most. 
