Table 2.1 demonstrates several important differences between the proposed work and existing 
studies. Unlike previous approaches that primarily relied on pressure insoles or VGRF foot 
sensors, the proposed system utilises bilateral ankle-mounted IMU sensors capable of 
capturing acceleration, rotational dynamics, asymmetry, and turning behaviour 
simultaneously. Furthermore, prior studies either lacked clinically interpretable feature 
engineering or relied solely on end-to-end deep learning models without explainability 
mechanisms. In contrast, the proposed framework combines clinically structured EGI features 
with machine learning architectures and integrates SHAP and attention-based explainability. 
Most importantly, previous studies commonly used segment-level or non-grouped validation 
strategies that may introduce subject-level data leakage, whereas this work applies strict 
Group 5-Fold subject-level cross-validation for more reliable clinical evaluation. 
It should be noted that the studies reviewed above were conducted on different datasets with 
varying sensor modalities, cohort characteristics, disease severity distributions, preprocessing 
pipelines, and validation strategies. While some studies used pressure insole or VGRF-based 
signals, others used full-body IMU systems or alternative gait datasets. Furthermore, no prior 
work was identified that trained and evaluated models under the exact experimental 
