Chapter 3 Methodology 
3.1 System Design 
The proposed system is a dual-branch machine learning pipeline for early-stage Parkinson’s 
Disease prediction using bilateral ankle-mounted Inertia Measurement Unit (IMU) data. The 
system is designed as an AI-based gait analysis system, capable of predicting gait instabilities 
that signify early-stage Parkinson’s disease (PD). It processes raw gait signals through 
preprocessing, feature extraction, machine learning and deep learning to generate 
classification and risk assessment outputs. The overall architecture of the system comprises 
of the following main components:  
1. Data obtained from wearable IMU sensors. 
2. Signal preprocessing and segmentation. 
3. Feature Extraction for early gait instability index (EGI) 
4. Machine learning and deep learning model training 
5. Explainable AI analysis 
6. Final prediction and performance evaluation 
The overall architecture is presented in figure 3.A and the detailed data preprocessing flow is 
illustrated in figure 3.B.  
15 
Fig 3.A: The system’s full architecture: Two parallel branches. 
Figure 3.A shows the full dual-branch system of obtaining raw data and evaluating clinical 
outcome. The pipeline consists of a shared preprocessing stage, followed by the EGI-SVM 
branch running on the left and the Deep Learning branch running on the right, both leading to the 
subject-level evaluation stage, as shown.  
The system loads bilateral ankle IMU recordings from 149 subjects, where 82 are healthy 
controls and 68 early-stage PD patients (Hoehn and Yahr stages 1-2); it is then sampled at 
100HZ across 12 channels (3-axis accelerometer and 3-axis gyroscope per ankle). The data is 
then passed through one common pre-processing pipeline, which later on splits into two 
separate branches of classifiers. 
16 
Fig 3.B: The Data Preprocessing Pipeline 
Figure 3.B illustrates the step by step data preprocessing pipeline. The common 
pre-processing pipeline goes through three consecutive steps. The first step is to partition the 
continuous recording into Walk windows and Turn windows based on the annotated event 
labels, resulting in only purposeful locomotion events included and non-gait transitions  and 
other events excluded from any window.. In the second stage, a per-channel 4th order 
zero-phase Butterworth bandpass filter (0.5-10 HZ) filters both the DC drift and high 
frequency noise per channel while retaining the gait frequency band (0.5-10 Hz) and the 
pathological resting tremor band (3-8 Hz).  This produces a common input of 12079 windows 
of size 200 x 12 with 75% overlap is provided to both branches as windows that are 
overlapping and labelled. Once the framework is preprocessed, it splits into two branches as 
depicted in Figure 3.A. 
In the proposed EGI (Early Gait Instability Index) feature extraction module window-shaped 
gait parameters for each are extracted as five clinically relevant component scores, namely 
17 
Variability, Asymmetry, Tremor, Timing and Turning, producing a 55-dimensional structured 
feature vector for every window. This feature matrix is used to train a Support Vector 
Machine (SVM) Classifier. Without structuring of EGI, eighty-four baseline raw IMU 
statistical and spectral features are used to train a parallel SVM baseline as a no-EGI baseline 
comparison to support the claim of discriminative value added by EGI. To get 
component-level attribution and generate clinical interpretation, the EGI-SVM model is 
analyzed using post-hoc SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations). 
The first part deals with the extraction of Engineered Early Gait Instability features and 
application of classical machine learning techniques such as Support Vector Machine(SVM). 
The second branch uses raw IMU signal windows directly as input to deep learning models 
such as 1D CNN, Hybrid CNN-Transformer and ResNet-based models. 
For the deep learning architectures, the raw 200×12 IMU windows are fed directly into the 
models without any hand-crafted feature engineering. The three models are as follows: 1) 
1D-CNN model with SE (Squeeze-and-Excitation) channel attention and residual blocks; 
2)Hybrid CNN-Transformer model that replaces Transformer layers with pre-layer norm 
transformations and includes a classification mechanism for the CLS (Classification) token; 
and 3) ResNet-18 model applied on 96×96 Recurrence Plot (RP) image representations of the 
IMU signals. The information provided from each event in the gait cycle for each of the 
Hybrid model's temporal regions is also extracted via attention map analysis to help 
determine the temporal regions of the gait cycle that are the most informative for 
classification. 
Both branches are evaluated at the subject level in a shared framework of 5-fold 
Cross-Validation ensuring that no subject is present in both training and test sets at the same 
time. For each model a classification threshold is determined optimally using Youden's J 
statistic. A model based Hoehn & Yahr stage-stratified analysis ensures to re-evaluate the 
best-performing model separately on H and Y Stage 1 and Stage 2 subgroups against healthy 
controls, and to measure clinical utility at the earliest detectable disease stages. 
