3.2 Software Components and Implementation 
A. Dataset 
The WearGait-PD dataset (Anderson et al., Scientific Data,2025) is obtained from the 
Synapse open access repository, https://www.synapse.org/Synapse:syn52540892/wiki/. The 
entire dataset comprises 185 subjects including 100 patients with PD and 85 healthy controls 
of similar age.  Data was programmatically accessed using the synapseclient library and 
downloaded using token-based authentication, as well as filtering the subjects prior to 
downloading by a set of clinical criteria. The following image shows the overview of data 
workflow for the WearGait-PD dataset; it is collected from Anderson et al., Scientific 
Data,2025. 
Fig 3.C: WearGait-PD data collection and processing workflow 
(https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-026-06806-2) 
Subject Selection Rationale: 
The Modified Hoehn & Yahr (H and Y) scale is a metric that describes PD severity on a scale 
of 0 (no sign of disease), 1 (disease in 1 hemisphere of the body), 1.5 (disease in 1 hemibody 
plus axial signs), 2 (disease in both hemibodies, no disability in posture), 2.5 (mild bilateral 
with recovery of posture), 3 (moderate bilateral with postural instability), 4 (severe, still 
ambulatory), and 5 (wheelchair-confined). It includes patients from H and Y Stage 1 through 
to Stage 3 and beyond as the whole data set mean is 2.15 ± 0.61. 
19 
This project only covers early stage PD ((H and Y) ≤ 2) -stages 1,1.5,2. The clinical and 
engineering rationale for this specific cohort selection is threefold: first, patients in early 
stages exhibit subtle, clinically ambiguous gait deviations that are very difficult to detect and 
classify using traditional visual assessments. Second, detecting PD at stages 3-5 provides 
limited clinical value since severe motor dysfunction is already visibly apparent to clinicians 
without technology and finally, early detection can make a big difference from a clinical 
standpoint since it will allow drug treatment to begin before further degeneration takes place. 
After applying the ((H and Y) ≤ 2) filter, 68 PD patients were retained. Of 85 available 
controls, 82 had complete valid recordings for both task files; 3 were excluded due to missing 
CSV data. The final cohort is 149 subjects — 68 PD, 82 controls. Each subject contributed 
two structured gait task recordings: 
SelfPace.csv – self-paced walking trial 
TUG.csv – Timed Up and Go test (walk, turn, return, sit) 
Each recording provides 12 IMU channels at 100 Hz from bilateral ankle sensors: 3-axis 
accelerometer and 3-axis gyroscope for both left and right ankles. Although the full 
WearGait-PD sensor suite covers 13 body locations, only bilateral ankle channels are used in 
this project. This design decision is supported by the biomechanical literature: ankle-mounted 
IMUs capture the complete gait cycle - heel strike, stance, toe-off, and swing phases - with 
the highest signal-to-noise ratio for step detection. Ankle sensors also capture tremor 
propagation to the lower limb and bilateral asymmetry directly from foot contact dynamics. 
