B. EDA 
EDA was performed before building any feature extraction or model to gain insights about 
the signal characteristics and confirm that the data is suitable for classification. 
Class distribution analysis showed that there was a slight imbalance (55% Control vs 45% PD 
at the window level). Considering that, this led to the use of inverse-frequency class 
weighting rather than over sampling in all classifiers, as oversampling time-series windows 
can lead to introducing temporal artifacts. These differences between PD and Control signals 
were confirmed by inspection of the raw signal from many subjects:  
● The magnitude of acceleration between two gait cycles is greater for PD subjects 
at the peak of the peak-to-peak range. 
● Increase in the power spectral density (PSD) within the tremor band (3-8Hz) is 
found in PD subjects when compared to controls. 
● For PD subjects, the left-right asymmetry for acceleration RMS is greater.  
These observations were evaluated using Mann Whitney U and Cohen's d tests. Key findings 
showed that the Variability component (d = +0.8, p < 0.001), Asymmetry (d = +0.6, p < 0.001), 
Timing (d = +0.5, p < 0.001) were statistically significant. The tremor component showed 
marginal but statistically significant separation (p=0.033, d= -0.044), however the small effect 
size indicated that it is a weak discriminator during walking. This  is physiologically expected 
since PD resting tremor is partially suppressed during voluntary walking through an action related 
inhibition mechanism. This EDA finding directly influenced the EGI design ; tremor is retained 
as a secondary rather than primary discriminator. 
A quality check of the signals confirmed no clipped values and demonstrated that the 
bandpass filtering successfully removed any DC offset. Furthermore, the low amount of 
missing data, comprising less than 2% of each recording, was effectively handled using linear 
interpolation. 
C. Pre-processing Pipeline 
The pre-processing pipeline was engineered in three ways: clinically relevant amplitude of 
the signals was preserved to extract features, non-gait noise was removed without altering the 
21 
frequency bands of the gait and tremor signals, and there was no data leakage into the 
cross-validation evaluation framework. 
A flowchart for data preprocessing pipeline: 
Raw CSV Files → Load and check required IMU columns → Linear interpolation of missing 
values → Butterworth Bandpass Filter → Event label segmentation(Extract walk and turn 
segments only)  → Sliding Window Segmentation → Quality check: reject windows with 
NaN values → Save: walk_windows, turn_windows. 
Missing Value Imputation: 
Linear interpolation was used rather than mean/median imputation because the IMU is a 
continuous time-series with samples that are tightly correlated from one to the next. Local 
linear trends are used to replace missing samples, which ensures the temporal continuity of 
the signal, while global mean imputation would cause artificial discontinuities in the signal 
and then affect the features in the frequency domain. 
Bandpass Filtering (0.5–10 Hz): 
A 4th order Butterworth filter was chosen for three engineering reasons: the passband of a 
Butterworth filter is maximally flat, meaning no ripple in the preserved frequency range is 
generated, unlike Chebyshev filters, the 4th order is sufficiently roll-off to allow the 
suppression of DC drift below 0.5 Hz and high frequency noise over 10 Hz without 
introducing excessive phase distortion, and lastly, zero phase filtering through the use 
forward-backward filter provides zero phase delay, which is critical since a phase shifts 
would lead to an incorrect alignment of the bilateral left-right signals and corrupt the 
asymmetry features that rely on precise time-domain synchrony between left and right ankle 
signals. 
The lower cutoff of 0.5 Hz is clinically grounded because it eliminates the DC offset and very 
slow drift. The upper cutoff of 10Hz includes the normal gait band of 0.5-3Hz, step frequency 
0.5-3steps/second and the PD resting tremor band (3-8Hz) with a 2Hz margin, and excludes 
sensor vibration and electrical noise above 10Hz. Most importantly, at this point, no 
amplitude normalisation is performed, as amplitude preservation is required because the 
features used, such as tremor power, gait power, RMS and variability range, are all amplitude 
dependent. These discriminative features would be lost during premature normalisation. 
22 
Event Segmentation: 
Walk events labelled Walk and SitToStand and Turn events labelled Turn and TurnToSit are 
extracted separately. Sitting, Standing and Unlabeled events are disregarded because they 
contain non gait motion that may introduce label noise in the classification process. Only data 
sections with a minimum length of 200 samples are retained. Shorter sections are ignored 
because they cannot form a complete window and may cause edge effects. 
Sliding Window Segmentation: 
A 2 second window (200 samples at 100 Hz) was selected as it would cover around 1.5-2 gait 
cycles at a typical cadence of walking (100-120 steps per minute), thus allowing the 
observation of the kinematic signature of one full gait cycle including stance and swing 
phases. With a 75% overlap, the number of training windows maximizes the amount of 
subject data without each window being isolated from the others, thus not losing the temporal 
continuity of gait patterns. This yields around 13-15 windows per gait segment.  
Per-channel z-score normalisation is intentionally performed in the inner loop of the 
cross-validation, using fit parameters for the training fold and testing with the test fold, in order to 
avoid any statistics based on test subjects from being used to directly influence the normalisation 
parameters. This is a crucial implementation choice that avoids a hidden type of data leakage 
which is often ignored in time series ML pipelines.  
D. Early Gait Instability Index Feature Extraction 
The Early Gait Instability Index (EGI) is the primary methodological contribution of this 
project. It is a framework for clinical feature engineering which extracts  61 features from 
every 200×12 IMU window and groups them into five clinically-motivated components, each 
of which is directly associated with a documented pathomechanical signature relevant to 
early-stage Parkinson's Disease. 
In contrast, the baseline SVM model uses a collection of handcrafted statistical and signal 
processing features computed directly from the raw IMU channels and derived magnitude 
signals. These include channel wise statistical measures (mean, standard deviation, RMS, 
range, IQR, skewness, and kurtosis), spectral power features, asymmetry measures, and jerk 
based smoothness descriptors. However, unlike EGI, these features are not structured into 
23 
clinically interpretable components linked to specific gait abnormalities associated with 
Parkinson’s Disease. There are three engineering justifications for EGI rather than this raw 
approach. First, EGI offers a clinical basis: each component represents a known PD gait 
abnormality thereby maintaining model behavior's interpretability for clinicians. Second, 
regarding the feature organisation, EGI provides a structured one through grouping the 
related measurements into clinically coherent components, which allows learning from 
physiologically meaningful representations instead of just a single statistical descriptor. 
Third, EGI enables feature importance explainability at the component level and makes it 
possible to explain feature importance in a clinically relevant manner, where feature sets are 
not that informative. 
Four bilateral magnitude signals are computed from the 12 raw IMU channels using the L2 
norm before computing any EGI component. Both Left and Right Acceleration Magnitudes 
are calculated as follows: 
L_acc = √((ch0^2) + (ch1^2) + (ch2^2)) 
R_acc = √((ch6^2) + (ch7^2) + (ch8^2)) 
and the magnitudes of the bilateral gyroscope as: 
L_gyr = √((ch3^2) + (ch4^2) + (ch5^2)) 
R_gyr = √((ch9^2) + (ch10^2) + (ch11^2)) 
These reduce the three spatial axes to a single signal of locomotion intensity for each ankle. The 
Z-axis gyroscope channels (L_gyr_z = ch5, R_gyr_z = ch11) are also kept for the Turning 
component because the vertical axis represents the main turning rotation. These magnitude 
signals are rotation-invariant and do not require fine-tuning due to small variations in sensor 
placement orientation across subjects, which is a crucial factor for the successful generalisation of 
these signals across subjects in clinical IMU datasets.  
The 61 features extracted by EGI are grouped into five components: Variability, Asymmetry, 
Tremor, Timing and Turning, described in detail below. 
Component 1- Variability 
Dopaminergic malfunction has been correlated with reduced rhythmic gait control, due to basal 
ganglia dysfunction, which results in an increase in stride to stride variability. This is one of the 
24 
earliest and most consistent kinetic features of Parkinson's Disease gait and can be observed at 
Hoehn and Yahr (H and Y) stage 1 and 2.  
The extraction of features for this component involves computation of 11 primary values. The 
main indicator used for assessment is the Coefficient of Variation (CV), which is computed 
separately for each accelerometer and gyroscope directions using the formula: CV = σ / (μ + 
ε), where ε = 1 × (10^-10) is introduced in order to avoid dividing by zero. Since this value is 
normalized, its use allows the comparison of variability between patients with different 
walking speeds and signal amplitudes. 
The following statistical descriptors of variability (complementary to max and min) are also 
calculated for L_acc and R_acc: peak-to-peak range (max- min), interquartile range (P75 - P25), 
and standard deviation. An addition of 18 directional features are computed by calculating the 
per- axis mean, standard derivation and RMS for the 6 acceleration channels (L_acc_x/y/z and 
R_acc_x/y/z), capturing directional gait characteristics that magnitude-based representations 
alone cannot provide. Together, these constitute the full 29 features of the variability component. 
The coefficient of variation varies from limb to limb and the variability score is the mean 
coefficient of variation in both the limbs: 
variability_score = (CV_L_acc + CV_R_acc) / 2.  
Component 2: Asymmetry 
PD is typically begins on one side at Stage 1 and then progresses on the same side, creating an 
ongoing left-right difference in kinematics that can be directly quantified by measuring the 
kinematics of the two ankles using bilateral sensors, and is the most clinically direct measure of 
early unilateral PD onset.  
Seven features are extracted. The primary measure is the Asymmetry Index, defined as a 
normalised percentage difference in signal magnitude: 
100 × |RMS_L − RMS_R| / (RMS_L + RMS_R + ε), 
where, ε is a constant to prevent the denominator from being zero. This normalised formulation 
eliminates any confounding effect by individual walking speed and effort, and ensures that the 
index is subject independent and directly comparable over the cohort.  
25 
Additional features include the absolute RMS difference (|RMS_L − RMS_R|), the RMS ratio 
(RMS_L / (RMS_R + ε)), the absolute mean difference (|μ_L − μ_R|) and the absolute standard 
deviation difference (|σ_L − σ_R|). Furthermore, L-R correlation coefficient (corrcoef(L_acc, 
R_acc)) quantifies the temporal synchrony of the two limbs; a high L-R correlation is evidence of 
high temporal synchrony between the movements of both limbs, which is characteristic of a 
healthy walker, while a low L-R correlation is characteristic of a PD patient, as their movement in 
one limb is impaired.  
Asymmetry index is set to be the component score: asymmetry_score = asym_index. 
Component 3: Tremor  
PD is also associated with resting tremor in the frequency band 3-6 Hz. Even though 
voluntary walking suppresses the presence of classical resting tremor through action 
inhibition mechanisms, subclinical high-frequency oscillatory activity remains in the lower 
limb while walking. In particular, higher levels of energy across the spectral band from 3 to 10 Hz 
compared with the normal gait frequency band 0.5–3 Hz yield a measurable signature of PD in 
the frequency domain, even when walking. 
A power spectral density (PSD) is calculated using scipy.signal.periodogram and eight features 
are extracted. Ankle tremor band power is calculated as the sum of PSD values in the range of 
3-10 Hz and gait band power is calculated in the 0.5-3 Hz range for each ankle. Thus, the ratio of 
the power of the tremor and the power of the gait is then defined as: 
tremor_ratio = tremor_power / (gait_power + ε), 
which is a measure of the increased amplitude of high frequency pathological activity compared 
to normal gait rhythm. 
The Freeze Index (FI), which is a composite measurement, is also calculated as the ratio of the 
total tremor power to total gait power in both the arms: 
FI = (tremor_power_L + tremor_power_R) / (gait_power_L + gait_power_R + ε) 
The above equation is derived based on previously described clinical measurements of Freezing 
of Gait (FOG). Finally, the mean ratio of bilateral tremors is considered as the third component 
measure: tremor_score = (tremor_ratio_L + tremor_ratio_R) / 2. 
Although this component has not yet been adequately separated from other components in the 
exploratory data analysis (Mann–Whitney U test, p > 0.05), it does not perform well on its own 
26 
during walking, as would be expected in a physiology of the suppression of resting tremor during 
voluntary motion. It is complementary, however, in the context of the overall EGI framework, 
with the provision of spectral information.  
Component 4: Timing 
The presence of Parkinson's disease disrupts the timing circuits in the basal ganglia, leading 
to impaired regularity and smoothness in gait, which results in diminished rhythmicity, an 
increase in the jerky nature of movements, and a broader distribution of spectral power. All of 
these can be quantitatively assessed using IMU signals. For this aspect, seven features are 
extracted. Spectral entropy is calculated as −Σ p(f) × log(p(f) + ε) based on the normalized 
power spectral density (PSD) of L_acc, where p(f) denotes the normalized distribution of the 
spectrum. This quantifies how signal power is spread across different frequencies; a healthy 
gait typically shows concentrated energy at the primary step frequency, leading to lower 
entropy, while gait in individuals with Parkinson's disease shows a more distributed spectral 
energy pattern, indicating irregular timing. 
Jerk root mean square (jerk_RMS) is the RMS value of the second derivative of L_acc, 
multiplied by the frequency squared (fs^2). Increased jerk values correspond to decreased 
smoothness of movements in PD. The smoothness index is defined as 1 / (1 + jerk_RMS), and is 
bounded for increasingly smooth motion, with higher values indicating smoother motion. Peak 
Jerk, which is also defined as the maximum absolute second derivative, is used to identify 
extreme transient movement irregularities. 
The regularity of stride is measured by the autocorrelation of L_acc at the first prominent peak 
after a 10 sample lag. The closer the value is to 1, the more periodic the gait is, and the closer to 
0, the more irregular the stepping pattern. The most dominant gait frequency is the one that has 
the highest PSD in the 0.5-3Hz range. 
The component score is computed as a scaled combination of the most discriminative temporal 
irregularity measures: 
timing_score = entropy/5 + jerk_rms/1000, 
in which the scaling factors are to make each term comparable in its numerical range.  
27 
Component 5: Turning  
Turning subtasks of the Timed UP and Go (TUG)  test is particularly impaired in PD. Patients 
usually show decreased peak angular velocity, multiple-step turning strategy, prolonged 
turning time, and bilateral gyroscope asymmetry. 
This component is computed only within Turn windows; all feature values are set to zero 
within Walk windows according to the is_turn flag. 
Eight features are extracted from the mean bilateral gyroscope value of Z axis, given by: 
gyr_z = (L_gyr_z + R_gyr_z) / 2. 
Four statistical measures such as mean, max, std, and peak-to-peak range of |gyr_z| are used 
to measure magnitude and variability of turning motion in terms of rotational angle. 
Rotational jerk is measured as the root-mean-square, RMS, of the first derivative of gyr_z 
scaled by the sampling frequency fs, where lower values correspond to smooth turning 
motion. Rotational smoothness index is given by: 
turn_smoothness = 1 / (1 + turn_jerk_rms). 
The difference in bilateral RMS of rotational gyroscope values is a measure of turning 
asymmetry between the left and right feet during the turning process and calculated as: 
|RMS(L_gyr_z) − RMS(R_gyr_z)| / (RMS_L + RMS_R + ε). 
Component score calculation formula includes four features and is given by: 
turning_score = 1.5 × (std/5 + jerk_rms/50 + asymmetry + range/10). 
These scaling coefficients (1.5, /5, /50, /10) are not learnable parameters; they are 
normalisation constants selected through experimentation for the purpose of ensuring that all 
features contribute equally, since otherwise a single feature may predominate within the 
composite score. 
Composite EGI Score 
The five scores are then summed up and normalized to form the final composite index of gait 
instability for each time window: 
28 
EGI = (variability_score + asymmetry_score/30 + tremor_score + timing_score + 
turning_score) / 5. 
The asymmetry score is normalized by dividing it by 30 before aggregation, since its unit of 
measure is percentages (0-100) and the other four components are in the range of 0-1 
approximately. Otherwise, the asymmetry component will dominate the aggregation process 
and make the others irrelevant. 
The is_turn flags  are removed from the feature set used for SVM training. The is_turn flag is 
not related to physiological measures but more to data provenance. Therefore, incorporating 
this flag will be a task-identifier that could potentially induce classification bias in the 
prediction model. Thus, the SVM is trained using only features derived from the EGI, as 
opposed to 110 features in the original model. 
E. Machine Learning and Deep Learning Models 
E1. SVM with EGI- Primary Classical Model 
A Support Vector Machine (SVM) with a radial function (RBF) kernel (C=10, gamma= 
‘scale’) was selected as the primary classical classifier. Compared with other models such as 
Random Forest, Logistic Regression, and k-Nearest Neighbours (k-NN), this algorithm works 
better with moderate-high dimensional features and few subjects, as margin maximization 
helps to generalize well and prevent subject-level overfitting. 
RBF kernel allows building non-linear decision boundaries without explicit feature 
expansion. Furthermore, inverse frequency class weight balancing is applied for mild class 
imbalance correction without oversampling in time series data. 
For implementation, sklearn.svm.SVC with probability=True and group 5-fold 
cross-validation are used. In each fold, missing values are imputed with column-wise median 
values from the training set. Then, standard scaling with StandardScaler is applied to the train 
set only to avoid data leakage. 
The probabilities obtained from each window for the subject are combined through averaging 
of all the windows associated with the same subject to get one PD probability measure for 
each subject. 
E2. SVM Baseline- Raw IMU without EGI 
29 
To measure the contribution of the proposed EGI architecture, a second SVM model using 
only raw statistical IMU features without clinical engineering is also trained. The features 
used for the baseline model include independent channel-wise statistical measures such as the 
mean, standard deviation, RMS, range, IQR, skewness, and kurtosis. In total, 110 features are 
extracted per window for the baseline model. 
This baseline acts as a benchmark for comparison between clinically engineered structured 
features and traditionally unstructured statistically extracted features using the same 
methodology.  
E3. 1D CNN with Residual and SE Attention Blocks 
A one-dimensional convolutional neural network (1D-CNN) working on the 200×12 raw 
IMU window signals transposed to 12×200 in accordance with the PyTorch channel-first 
convention was chosen as the first deep learning model for training due to the fact that a 
convolutional network will be able to detect the temporal patterns of gaits automatically, 
without any manual feature selection process. 
The architecture involves four residual blocks with increasing filter sizes from 64 to 256, 
while the kernel sizes increase from 7 to 3. Skip connections (He et al., 2016) facilitate stable 
deep convolutional networks by enabling gradient flow in the network. After each residual 
block, there is an SE (Squeeze-and-Excitation) attention mechanism (Hu et al., 2018), which 
enables channel-wise re-scaling and learning the relevance of the different IMU channels for 
the network. This is especially important for gaits' analysis, as the ankle accelerometer and 
gyroscope have distinct significance for locomotion characterization. 
Focal Loss, 2.0 is chosen instead of classical cross-entropy because the former gives more 
weight to hard or ambiguous training samples while down-weighting easy cases to minimize 
losses due to overfitting. 
Gaussian noise injection (σ=0.05), amplitude scaling with a uniform distribution between 0.9 
and 1.1, time-shifting +/-10 samples, and channel dropout, 0.1, augmentation techniques are 
used only in the train phase. They introduce natural variations for a realistic situation but do 
not affect class labels. 
A linear warm-up for 5 epochs is applied before cosine learning rate decay to ensure better 
initial convergence. Furthermore, there is a 1.6×PD class-weight boost in combination with 
30 
frequency-based weights in favour of sensitivity, which is more clinically valuable than 
specificity. Finally, early stopping with patience=15 is used to get the optimal model in terms 
of validation-AUC. 
E4. Hybrid CNN-Transformer Model 
While the 1D-CNN is able to well capture the local temporal gait patterns, it has limited 
capability for the model of the long-range dependencies over the entire gait sequence. To address 
such a drawback, a hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture is proposed. 
The raw 200×12 IMU sequence is initially processed with a 3-block CNN encoder with SE 
attention, which cuts the length of the IMU sequence from 200 to 50 and expands the feature 
dimension from 12 to 128. This enables the Transformer module to learn to understand the 
higher-level temporal relationships, avoiding the need for low-level signal extraction. 
A learnable CLS token is attached to the sequence in front of Transformer encoding. The CLS 
token is used for self-attention to fuse the global sequence-level information for final 
classification. 
Pre-LayerNorm (Pre-LN) is employed in the Transformer layers to overcome the problems of 
standard Post-LN, as it has been proven to be better for optimisation stability and gradient 
propagation for smaller datasets. The final architecture is composed of 4 attention heads and 2 
Transformer layers, which is a trade-off of modelling capacity and risk of overfitting. 
The final model also features data augmentation, learning-rate warmup, focal loss and extended 
early stopping for better generalisation.  
E5. ResNet-18 with Recurrence Plot Representation 
The ResNet-18 model consists of a novel approach to gait classification, which transforms every 
IMU window into a Recurrence Plot (RP) image and subsequently classifies it as a 2D image 
classification problem. RPs store temporal gait features such as periodicity, regularity, complexity 
in a spatial representation which can be analysed by the trained CNN feature detectors.  
Gaussian-like thresholding has been applied rather than binary threshold to retain graded 
similarity information. 
The bilateral asymmetry of the accelerometers (|L_acc − R_acc|) is captured in Channel 1, the 
average of the bilateral accelerometers (L_acc + R_acc)/2 in Channel 2, and the mean of the 
31 
Z-axis of the bilateral gyroscope in Channel 3, resulting in each window being transformed into a 
3 channel 96×96 RP image. 
Because there are not enough windows to train an 18-layer CNN from scratch, the pretrained 
ImageNet CNN is used for transfer learning. The last fully connected layer is followed by a 
custom classification head (512→256→BatchNorm→GELU→Dropout→2). By using the 
Mixed-precision training, which includes torch.cuda.amp.autocast, the memory consumption on 
the GPU can be lower and the training speed can be faster.  
F. Evaluation Framework 
Group 5-Fold Cross Validation has been used for all five models. Standard k-fold randomly 
distributes individual windows among folds, thus enabling that some windows may be present in 
both training and test sets, even if they come from the same subject. This means that the model 
ends up learning subject-specific gait signatures instead of generalising PD patterns, which is a 
severe degree of data leakage that overestimates reported values. GroupKFold puts all windows 
of a specific subject into only one of the folds, with 100% subject-level separation. 
The window-level probabilities are averaged over each test fold to calculate a single subject-level 
PD probability for each fold, which represents a clinically realistic case where a decision is made 
on a patient level rather than a 2-second window level. So the optimal decision threshold is 
selected using the Youden's J statistic (J = Sensitivity + Specificity − 1) on the ROC curve at the 
subject level, instead of the default threshold of 0.5, the statistically correct method for 
imbalanced binary medical classification that maximises sensitivity and specificity at the same 
time. Bootstraps with 1000 resamples are calculated for AUC to measure the estimation 
uncertainty in the H and Y Stage 1 subgroup (n = 10).  
G. Feature Selection, Ablation, and Attention-Based Interpretability 
The EGI ablation study removes each of the five component groups one at a time, and the 
SVM is trained again with the same Group 5-fold cross validation, while the drop in AUC is 
recorded.The component that leads to the biggest drop in the value of AUC when removed is 
considered the one carrying maximum information, thereby validating the notion that the 
constructed groups complement each other discriminatively. 
32 
Approximate Shapley values of the SVM probability output are computed using SHAP 
KernelExplainer with k=100 centroids derived from k-means summarisation of the training set, 
evaluated on 300 test windows, of which 150 are PD and 150 Control. To represent the training 
distribution with the summarised distribution without significantly increasing the computational 
cost, k-means summarisation is used instead of random sampling. The feature-level SHAP values 
are then combined within each EGI component group to provide component-level attribution, 
which allows the understanding of the impact of engineered gait mechanisms on classification 
performance. 
Attention based interpretability is also taken out of the hybrid CNN–Transformer model in 
addition to the post hoc attribution.Besides post hoc explanation, attention based interpretability 
is extracted from the hybrid CNN–Transformer model for further analysis of the internal 
representation learning. The CLS token is used to get temporal attention in both the 
Pre-LayerNorm transformer layers and the CLS token is the one that gathers information from 
every position in the sequence. The CLS-attention weights are separated in every layer and then 
averaged across the layers by a geometric mean for Parkinson's and control subjects to gain a 
stable temporal attention profile throughout the gait cycle. 
Temporal attention differences between classes are quantified using a Mann–Whitney U-test at 
each gait phase, which determines the gait intervals with a statistically significant difference 
between the classes in their attention allocation. This yields a statistical characterization of 
discriminative temporal focus learned by the model, as a function of its position. 
The Feature-level attention is obtained from Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) blocks in the CNN 
encoder that produce channel-wise gating weights on the 128-dimensional feature representation. 
These weights are the results of adaptive re-calibration of the IMU-derived feature channels. To 
determine consistently emphasized feature dimensions, mean SE weights are calculated for PD 
and control groups and compared. 
SHAP, ablation and attention mechanisms combine to give a multi-layer interpretability 
framework. The post-hoc feature and component attribution computed by SHAP, the causal 
evaluation of engineered feature group importance computed by ablation, and the model's 
attention mechanisms revealed the model's temporal and feature-level focus. Uniformity of these 
views enhances the trust in patterns learned from data and increases the interpretability of both 
engineered and deep learned representations.  
33 
H. Clinical Stage-Stratified Evaluation Using Hoehn and Yahr 
The Hoehn and Yahr (H and Y) stratification analysis evaluates the best performing model, 
the Hybrid CNN-Transformer, on clinically meaningful disease stages. The model is tested on 
H and Y Stage 1 vs Control, Stage 2 vs Control in addition to its performance on the full 
dataset.  
During subject matching, a case sensitivity mismatch between subject IDs in the 
preprocessed numpy arrays (lowercase) and the clinical CSV (uppercase) was resolved by 
converting both sides to  lowercase before matching, which ensured all 68 PD subjects were 
correctly linked to their clinical metadata. 
H and Y score was then obtained from the clinical metadata and divided into two categories: 
stage 1, representing unilateral motor dysfunction with minimal functional impairment, and 
stage 2, representing early bilateral involvement without significant postural impairment. 
The model was tested in three experimental settings under identical Group 5-fold 
cross-validation procedure with regard to the following groups –  
1. all patients diagnosed with Parkinson’s disease vs. control group, 
2.  Stage 1 Parkinson’s disease vs. control group,  
3. Stage 2 Parkinson’s disease vs. control group.  
All experiments were carried out with the same model architecture, preprocessing pipeline, 
and hyperparameters configuration. Given that there is only a small number of Stage 1 
patients, the bootstrap method with 1,000 samples was used to calculate the 95% confidence 
intervals of AUC metrics. 
The results obtained through this stratified approach showcase the capabilities of the 
algorithm at various levels of severity of the disease, proving its efficiency in recognizing 
Parkinson’s disease at the earliest stages of development. 
