Analysis of component scores: 
The Variability Score showed a significant negative effect (d = −0.233, p < 0.001), indicating 
that individuals with PD have a lower degree of variability in step length than individuals 
without PD. This is surprising, as one would expect persons with PD to have greater 
variability in step length; however, due to stiffness in the motor system associated with PD, 
persons with PD will have a consistently and uniformly abnormal gait rather than a normal 
and healthy gait. 
The Asymmetry Score was also statistically significant (d = −0.127, p < 0.001) and identified 
individuals with PD to have less acceleration between their left and right ankles. The 
asymmetrical gait pattern corresponds with the clinical observation that individuals with PD 
may initially present with greater clinical signs of motor disability on one side of their body, 
thereby displaying a statistically significant difference in acceleration between their two legs. 
The Tremor Score of the composite measure was found to have a small but statistically 
significant difference (d = −0.044, p = 0.033). This finding is not surprising because, when 
people with PD are actively walking or performing another activity that inhibits them from 
using their strength to walk (like resting), they will not have any tremors to measure on an 
ankle sensor while walking. However, when the EGI was developed, the SHAP analysis 
demonstrated that there are still some subtle frequency domain shifts in resting tremor that 
can be attributed to the tremor score with a contribution of 13.4% of the predictive signal for 
the EGI. 
The Timing Score had the largest positive effect (d = +0.274, p < 0.001), indicating that 
individuals with PD have more irregular and jerky movement patterns than controls and are 
more unpredictable in their movement patterns. This finding corresponds to the established 
clinical observation that the basal ganglia are responsible for the rhythm and smoothness of 
movement, and therefore disruption to the basal ganglia in PD would result in a 
corresponding disruption of rhythm and smoothness in gait. 
The Turning Score, which was based entirely on turning events, demonstrated a statistically 
significant difference (d = +0.072, p = 0.028) in that the group of PD patients showed greater 
angular velocity irregularity when they turned. 
