4.3 Experiment 1: SVM Without EGI (Raw IMU Baseline) 
Setup: We collected 110 features from raw motion sensor data, comprising seven 
channel-wise statistical measures such as mean, standard deviation, RMS, range, 
IQR,skewness, and kurtosis. Input features such as frequency, smoothness, asymmetry of the 
left and right side of the body, average, variance, and peak were used for an algorithm known 
as Support Vector Machine (SVM) in order to classify different patients. In order to eliminate 
any potential bias, none of the data points of any patient were included in both training and 
testing processes, which was achieved via the application of 5 fold cross validation. The 
decision threshold point of the classification process was optimized using Youden's J criterion 
in order to maximize sensitivity and specificity simultaneously. The prediction scores of 
patients were computed using an additional scheme for all segments of their data points. 
This baseline was designed to evaluate the contribution of EGI framework by comparing 
clinically organised features against unstructured raw statistical descriptors using the same 
classifier. 
