● Because there were only 10 PD subjects in the Stage 1 study population, the AUC 
estimate is inherently unstable; thus, the 95% bootstrap confidence interval ([0.740, 
0.947]) should be interpreted cautiously due to the large range within the interval. 
● The F1 score of 0.5143 in Stage 1 reflects the extreme class imbalance (10 cases of 
PD vs. 81 controls) rather than poor model performance. The F1 is a mathematical 
product of the unbalanced distribution of use of cases of PD; due to the imbalanced 
distribution, the high sensitivity (90.0%) and high specificity (80.2%) found with this 
model demonstrate the model's utility for detecting early PD, but have resulted in a 
low F1 score. 
● In Stage 1, 90% sensitivity has significant clinical implications since it suggests that 9 
of every 10 early-stage PD patients will be identified by the wearable ankle sensor 
system and that there is significant emerging evidence to support the clinical utility of 
using this sensor system for detecting early-stage PD. 
Clinical Implication 
● The consistent and detectable interlimb gait asymmetries observed in patients with 
Stage 1 PD would suggest that wearable ankle sensors will provide support for an 
earlier clinical diagnosis of PD before the emergence of bilateral symptoms. 
66 
● Because the deviations in gait patterns in patients with Stage 1 are structured, it may 
be possible to create a symptom-specific, individualised treatment plan and 
rehabilitation goals from the wearable ankle sensor data. 
● These data highlight the value of evaluating PD by stage: combining all stages of PD 
together (AUC 0.7761) obscures the significantly superior performance of the model 
when evaluating early-stage patients, while testing for similarly low sensitivity and 
specificity in later-stage PD patients.  
