4.12 Comparison with Related Works 
Because the referred works were assessed utilizing various datasets, sensor setups, 
preprocessing techniques, subject demographics, and validation methodologies, cross-study 
numerical comparisons should be regarded with caution. Additionally, a number of earlier 
research employed segment-level evaluation techniques or mixed-stage Parkinson's groups, 
which may result in higher apparent performance because of simpler categorization settings 
or possible subject-level data leakage. The suggested approach, on the other hand, 
implements stringent Group 5-Fold subject-level cross-validation to guarantee realistic 
generalization performance and concentrates exclusively on early-stage Parkinson's disease 
(H and Y ≤ 2). Therefore, rather than asserting absolute metric superiority, the comparison is 
meant to place the suggested framework within the larger PD gait-analysis literature.  
The best-performing models from this study are directly compared to three closely 
comparable studies found in the literature review: Nanayakkara et al. [2], Mostafavi et al. [3], 
and Naimi et al. [4] in order to benchmark the experimental results. Three systematic 
comparisons are provided:  
(i) metrics for classification performance across all models and related works (Table 4.15);  
(ii) evaluation technique and important design decisions (Table 4.16); and  
(iii) improvement over the raw SVM baseline across suggested models (Table 4.17). 
A crucial warning applies to all cross-study comparisons: variations in dataset, sensor 
modality, subject group, PD severity stage, and evaluation technique prevent direct numerical 
comparison. The analysis that follows specifically addresses these distinctions. According to 
Tables 4.5–4.11, subject-level Youden's J threshold results are used in all proposed-work 
figures. 
