Chapter 5 Impacts of the Project 
5.1 Impact of this project on societal, health, safety, legal and cultural 
issues  
Patients with Parkinson's Disease suffer greatly due to this disorder. However, this burden 
extends beyond just the patient and includes caregivers and healthcare systems as well. With 
an early diagnosis of Parkinson’s Disease before significant motor limitations, the patient is 
able to receive earlier treatments through different methods such as pharmacologic (levodopa 
therapy), physiotherapy, and lifestyle modifications. All of these treatments have 
demonstrated positive effects on the disease; including the slowing of disease progression and 
improving the quality of life of the patient. The Pipeline for the early detection of PD will 
provide a method for screening due to its objective, low-cost and non-invasive nature and can 
be used by primary care providers. This is particularly important for Low/Middle-Income 
Countries that do not have access to specialists in neurology. 
From a safety viewpoint, if PD-related gait abnormalities are detected early enough this will 
allow for early intervention to improve balance and ultimately decrease the risk of falling as a 
result of balance issues. From a cultural perspective, by framing detection as a traditional 
screening device it will help reduce the social stigma associated with a PD diagnosis. Finally, 
the legal considerations for protecting patient data must also be met. To accomplish this the 
IMU sensor data will have to be securely stored and de-identified prior to any real-world use. 
5.2 Impact of this project on environment and sustainability 
Our project uses low-power wearable IMU sensors that consume very little electricity and do 
not need chemical reagents or lab supplies. Unlike DaTscan imaging, which uses radioactive 
tracers and specialized equipment, the IMU-based pipeline has a very small impact on the 
environment. Training the model with cloud-based GPU computing (Kaggle) does have a 
carbon cost, but this is only a one-time expense for training. Inference can be done on locally 
collected sensor data on edge devices that use very little power, making the system 
sustainable on a larger scale. 
