Chapter 8 Conclusions 
8.1 Summary 
This study has established and tested the Early Gait Instability Index (EGI) framework for the 
purpose of detecting early-stage Parkinson's Disease using bilateral ankle IMU sensor data. 
The EGI framework takes raw IMU data and translates it into five clinically validated 
composite dimensions (variability, asymmetry, tremor, timing and turning,) thus offering an 
understandable and organized alternative to the currently used method of extracting statistical 
features from unstructured data sources. Training an SVM on the EGI-based features resulted 
in a significant increase in predictive ability compared to using only the raw IMU-derived 
data as features; supporting that the use of the EGI framework provides a meaningful 
contribution to clinical practice. SHAP explainability showed support of this by indicating 
that all five of the EGI dimensions were relevant for classifying patients, as well as that no 
one of these dimensions dominated classification results. 
Three separate deep learning (DL) architectures were developed and assessed: (1) An 
improved 1D convolutional neural network (CNN) based architecture (whose convolutional 
building block includes a residual connection and squeeze-and-excitation (SE) attention 
mechanism), (2) A hybrid-cnn/transformer architecture (which employs a pre-layer 
normalization and augmentation transform) and (3) A convolutional neural network built on 
the ResNet-18 architecture, which was trained using two-dimensional (2-dimensional) 
Recurrence Plot representations of the gait windows. All three DL architectures were 
evaluated using a subject-level group cross-validation scheme consisting of five folds per 
group for a total of 25 different subjects to thoroughly estimate each model's performance 
without risk of data leakage. The Hybrid CNN-Transformer outperformed the 1D-CNN, 
ResNet-18 with Recurrence Plots, and both SVM baselines, achieving the highest values 
across all important assessment metrics: AUC of 0.8544, accuracy of 79.87%, sensitivity of 
80.88%, specificity of 79.01%, and F1-score of 0.7857.  
8.2 Limitations 
The first limitation is that while the WearGait-PD dataset is focused on the early stages of 
PD, the total number of participants in this dataset is limited and therefore limits the 
statistical power of cross-validated estimates and may affect their generalizability across 
other populations. Third, the generation of 2D Recurrence Plots requires significant 
79 
computational resources, which could create challenges for real-time processing on edge 
devices. Fourth, the current system does not include medication status (ON/OFF) for 
levodopa use which has an impact on gait in patients with PD. Finally, the testing of the 
pipeline has not been conducted in a prospective clinical trial setting. 
8.3 Future Improvement 
There are various pathways for future research. For instance, if researchers were to collect a 
larger multi-centered dataset consisting of early-stage PD patients, our model would be more 
broadly applicable and allow for subgroup analyses based on the H and Y stage and 
medication state. Additionally, the ResNet-18 RP model could be compressed through the use 
of quantization or knowledge distillation in order to deploy it on low-powered edge devices 
(e.g., Raspberry Pi, mobile phone). Another example would be utilizing continual learning 
techniques to personalize the model to individual patients over time and track the progression 
of the disease. Moreover, additional sensor modalities e.g., wrist-based accelerometers or 
pressure insoles, could offer even more discriminative ability to classify patients with PD. 
Lastly, a multi-site clinical validation study between neurologists would need to be done 
before any deployment into the real world. 
